A||||7||fieroluke||Engine problem that's driving me mad!||||8||
Z||000000||fieroluke||11-12-2001||05:37 PM||||Hi all,<P>you may or may not remember my problem with my recently rebuilt 87 4 banger. Please bear with me as I describe the symptoms in detail, <BR>maybe you can give me a hint. I'm about to hold a match to this car to see how long it burns! But here goes:<P>After a few hundred miles, a rocker bolt snapped off, and after replacing it, I got a<BR>ticking noise, which got less after the engine warmed up. Eventually, after a few days, the new rocker bolt would snap as well.<P>The new bolts would last fewer and fewer days, until the rocker arm cracked.<P>Turned out that the rocker arm cracked because the pushrod was bent. I guess the pushrod got bent more and more over time, because of the increasing gap. These impacts<BR>must have weakened the bolts/rocker arm respectively until the weaker of them snapped. Only  question remaining is why<BR>the pushrod got bent in the first place.<P>Well, the new valves were mated to the head<BR>which caused the valve to sit 0.4mm higher than the others (at most). Since the rockers on this engine can't be adjusted, this may<BR>have caused coil binding (the spring is compressed almost solid, but it's hard to<BR>tell because the oil shield or whatever it is<BR>obscures the view). There are no shims or<BR>anything under the springs.<P>So I installed a stud/nut setup like on the V6 to have an adjustable valve lash.<P>Yesterday I rotated the crank until the valve<BR>was fully closed, and tightened the nut to zero lash, and then 3/4 of a turn. I could still rotate the pushrod with my fingers.<P>I started the engine with the valve cover off, and alas, it was clicking again! Turned<BR>off the engine, and found the rocker arm was<BR>pretty loose. So I started the engine again,<BR>and tightened the rocker arm nut, and the ticking went away after about 1.5 turns<BR>(about 1mm, since this is not a GM stud but<BR>one I had a friend make for me on a lathe).<BR>When I loosened the nut just a little bit,<BR>the ticking came back.<P>But when I turned off the engine, I could<BR>no longer rotate the pushrod, even with the<BR>valve fully closed. Is this reason for concern? I don't know. Anyway, if I tighten<BR>more, I'm afraid of coil bind, if I tighten<BR>less, I'll get a ticking and probably a<BR>eventually a bent pushrod again.<P>Since the oil was spewing everywhere at this point, I stopped right there, and decided to try again tonight after work.<P>Started the engine, and right before it came<BR>to life, I felt a "puff" of air near my<BR>problem valve, accompanied by a hissing<BR>noise and a funny smell. Is this normal?<BR>It didn't happen again anyway. The engine started right up, and was not noisy.<P>So I put the valve cover back on, and assembled the air cleaner again. Now I get<BR>a clacking noise (not ticking) at a slow<BR>frequency. Slower than the valvetrain anyway.<BR>With the engine at 1200 rpm, I get the noise<BR>maybe 4-5 times per second.<P>Now, 1200rpm means 20 revs per second, and since the crank rotates twice per combustion,<BR>the valve opens 10 times per second, right?<BR>Why would I get a clicking noise every *second* ignition? I can even see the valvetrain move faster than the ticking noise, so I don't think that's it. But that's<BR>the only thing I touched. <P>Can anyone make any sense of this?<P>This is our most desparate hour. Help me, Obi-Wan Kenobi, you're my only hope...<BR>Or something along those lines...<P>-Oliver<P><BR>The springs were purchased new from the Fiero Store, and the other springs don't cause a problem.||||reg||8
Z||000001||StuGood||11-12-2001||06:44 PM||||First of all, remember I'm no Duke expert! This is probably way out of Stu's league.  Keeping that in mind...<BR><B>...new bolts would last fewer and fewer days, until the rocker arm cracked.  Turned out that the rocker arm cracked because the pushrod was bent...I guess the pushrod got bent more and more over time, because of the increasing gap...Only question remaining is why the pushrod got bent in the first place.<BR></B>I don't think that "the rocker cracked because the pushrod got bent."  Sounds more like the pushrod bent because the valvetrain is binding - my guess is the valve doesn't like to move - hitting something?   <BR><B>...I installed a stud/nut setup like on the V6 to have an adjustable valve lash.<BR>Yesterday I rotated the crank until the valve<BR>was fully closed, and tightened the nut to zero lash, and then 3/4 of a turn. I could still rotate the pushrod with my fingers.</B>Sounds like you have too much lash at this point, to me.<P><B>...started the engine with the valve cover off, and alas, it was clicking again!</B>(Same as above) <P><B>Turned off the engine, and found the rocker arm was pretty loose.</B>(Same as above).<P><B>So I started the engine again, and tightened the rocker arm nut, and the ticking went away...When I loosened the nut just a little bit, the ticking came back.<BR>But when I turned off the engine, I could<BR>no longer rotate the pushrod, even with the<BR>valve fully closed. Is this reason for concern?</B>I don't think so. At that point, I think you had zero lash.  You could tighten it down the recommended amount to center the lifter, I think - <B>if</B> everything else is normal/functioning.<P><B>...(if I tighten more)I'm afraid of coil bind, if I tighten less, I'll get a ticking and probably a eventually a bent pushrod again.</B>You might have trouble, if the lifter is completely collapsed, and you  effectively have a solid lifter.  Maybe the lifter is defective, or isn't getting oil for some reason?<P>I'd be tempted to remove the valve spring and see if the valve operates freely (don't let it drop in too far!).  But really I'm just guessing.  At least maybe someone with a clue will have some good ideas.  BTW, is it an intake or and exhaust valve giving trouble? <P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fierowrecker||11-12-2001||07:49 PM||||Use the Force Luke!<BR>Couldn't resist!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>At this point, I would suggest starting over...<BR>Pull the head and look at the piston top, you want to see if the valve has touched the piston...<BR>Also pull the cover for the lifters and remove the suspect lifter and see if it is colapsed or weak...<BR>You should be able to use a pushrod and push in the inside of the lifter, but it should take some force (I knew I would get to that!)...<BR>If the lifter seems to be strong (compaire to another lifter), put it in a pan of oil, and see if it pumps oil with the pushrod...<P>Another thing, is it an intake or exhaust valve?<BR>If it is an intake, does it have a rotator by misteak? Only an exhaust valve gets a rotator...<BR>If it is an exhaust valve, is the rotator intact? Sometimes they break and hit the valve guide...<P>Sence it is only one valve giving you greaf, even with the adjustment mod, I suspect it is hitting the guide and not the piston, but till you look, you can't tell...<P>E-mail me if you need further help!<P>------------------<BR>crash... The Fierowrecker<BR>         <IMG SRC="http://fiero.nl/uploads/crash_sig.jpg">        <IMG SRC="http://fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||fieroluke||11-13-2001||05:28 AM||||Originally posted by StuGood:<BR><B>I don't think that "the rocker cracked because the pushrod got bent."  Sounds more like the pushrod bent because the valvetrain is binding - my guess is the valve doesn't like to move - hitting something?</B><P>The rocker definitely broke because the pushrod was hitting it sideways. I can post<BR>a picture if I can still find it in the trash. My only explanation for the bolts<BR>lasting fewer and fewer miles is that the rod<BR>got increasingly bent.<P><BR>&gt;&gt;I could still rotate the pushrod with my fingers.<B>Sounds like you have too much lash at this point, to me.</B><BR>Well, I can rotate each pushrod when the valve is closed, both on the V6 and L4 engines.<P><B>I don't think so. At that point, I think you had zero lash.  You could tighten it down the recommended amount to center the lifter, I think - if everything else is normal/functioning.</B><BR>Maybe a lifter really is collapsed. Then I'd tighten the rocker down until the clicking<BR>goes away, but in effect I'd only be making the lifter solid.<P><B>You might have trouble, if the lifter is completely collapsed, and you  effectively have a solid lifter.  Maybe the lifter is defective, or isn't getting oil for some reason?</B><BR>It's definitely pumping oil, it was spewing out through the hole in the rocker arm.<BR>Although it took a while to do so (maybe 30 seconds or so).<BR>The lifter may be collapsed, although it has fewer than 1000 miles on its back.<P><B>I'd be tempted to remove the valve spring and see if the valve operates freely (don't let it drop in too far!).</B><BR>Don't have the tools for that. But the only resistance I can think of is if the coil binds.<P><B>BTW, is it an intake or and exhaust valve giving trouble? <BR></B><P>It's the leftmost valve, I think it is intake, but I'd have to check.<P>Thanks,<P>-Oliver<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||fieroluke||11-13-2001||05:35 AM||||Originally posted by Fierowrecker:<BR><B>Use the Force Luke!<BR>Couldn't resist!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"></B><BR>Oh, I'm about to use force alright ;-)<P><B>Pull the head and look at the piston top, you want to see if the valve has touched the piston...</B><BR>I'd like to avoid pulling the head if I can.<BR>It's messy...<P><B>Also pull the cover for the lifters and remove the suspect lifter and see if it is colapsed or weak...</B><BR>I'll check the lifter by pushing down on the pushrod (as I found a description on the web).<BR>I shouldn't be able to compress it more than 1mm, or it is bad, correct?<P><B>If the lifter seems to be strong (compaire to another lifter), put it in a pan of oil, and see if it pumps oil with the pushrod...</B><BR>It does pump oil alright. Does that mean it's good?<P><B>Another thing, is it an intake or exhaust valve?</B><P>It's the leftmost valve, I think that makes it an intake valve.<P><B>If it is an intake, does it have a rotator by misteak? Only an exhaust valve gets a rotator...</B><BR>How do I tell? My dad rebuilt the head...<P>Also, what about that blowby that occurred once? Maybe (assuming the lifter is<BR>collapsed and the valve is not fully closed) the exhaust gases found their way through the<BR>valve stem seal? <P><B>If it is an exhaust valve, is the rotator intact? Sometimes they break and hit the valve guide...</B><BR>See above: what does it look like and how do I tell?<P><B>Sence it is only one valve giving you greaf, even with the adjustment mod, I suspect it is hitting the guide and not the piston, but till you look, you can't tell...</B><P>Thanks,<P>-Oliver<P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by fieroluke (edited 11-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||11-13-2001||01:40 PM||||Oliver,<P>They make stoppers that clip on the rockers so you can work on the valves and not get showered with oil. You should be able to get them thru allot of parts stores. They are pretty much one size fits all. You can see one supplier at <A HREF="http://www.lislecorp.com/" TARGET=_blank>the Lisle tools site.</A><P>I'm not real sure what else I can tell you. Hard to say w/o seeing it.<P>Valve or lifter bind still sounds like the problem... A push rod won't bend from being loose. Same for breaking the rocker/stud.<P>A lifter can still pump oil and bind. Pumping oil just means it's not locked totally solid. <P>One question just to make sure.... Did the motor have pushrod guides and were they reinstalled? If they are missing then the rockers won't be at the right height. If one of the guides is baddly crushed thin it could also skew pivot position enough to be trouble.<P>Another issue is the installed height of the valves. ALLdata says 1.69 inch. I beleive that's measured from the spring seat to the top of the stem. If a valve is too tall then a lifter would bind, too short like binds a spring. Because the OE setup is not adjustable that height is even more critical.<P>Unfortunatly, there is no procedure for what you are doing with the valve studs. If you end up with a slight valve noise but a running motor it's not entirely bad. The 87 valves were built inherantly noisy. Over tightening them to stop that noise isn't a good thing.<P>I don't know what you made for studs... I'm guessing you did something to insure the position of the rocker ball...<P>Also keep in mind exhaust valves grow considerably when hot. If you look at valve lash settings for solid lifter motors you'll see the exhaust almost always gets more clearance than intake. With that in mind, the intake and exhaust on a hydraulic setup will likely also get adjusted a little diferant in your setup. (Having the exhaust to tight could would bind likely bind the lifter when hot.)<P>The only example I have handy... Ford 2.0 liter OHC motor with solid rockers. (Non hydraulic valve setup.)  Intake lash is .008 inch exhaust is .010 inch.<P>The left most valve is, I think, an intake valve.<P>The really slow ticking noise sounds like you could have an ignition arc. See if it shows sparks someplace in the dark... <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000006||rogergarrison||11-13-2001||05:09 PM||||It kind of sounds to me like a defective or collapsed lifter. You can tighten it to get the noise out but its bottomed out and no hydrolic action. putting too much pressure on the pushrod. Pontiac has always had weak pushrods. Ive bent many of them thru the years reving the engine tite. Anyway, id look at the lifters first. If there new, that still dont mean there ok. Might try swapping it with another cyl and see what happens. if that cyl now messes up, buy a new set.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Indiana_resto_guy||11-13-2001||06:37 PM||||If it were me trying your setup, I would try to judge and compare by feeling the other push rods. Adjust your setup to the same sort of feel and measure the distance below the rocker and shim under it. Then revert back to the original stuff and see what happens.<P>My $0.02||||reg||1
